Researchers have found that this bug eats plastic! There is new hope for solving the pollution problem
They lay their eggs in the hives, and as soon as they hatch, the insects grow by feeding on beeswax. Professor Bertocchini removed the larvae from the hive and stored them in a plastic bag, which he later found riddled with holes.
One reason the insects are able to break down plastic at such a rapid rate may be that they eat beeswax in the first place, the report said. Beeswax has a similar chemical structure to polyethylene and Prof Bertocchini calls it a “natural plastic”.
