Disposable plastics permeate almost every aspect of our lives: from disposable coffee cups or straws in smoothies you picked up on the way to work, to hidden fibers woven into wet wipes and tiny shimmering fragments in cosmetics.
Some of them are compostable, which means that they can not only be broken down by microorganisms, but can also be converted into compost along with food and other organic waste. Only a small portion of these plastics are household compostable, so the “compostable” label usually means industrial compostable.
Compostable packaging has European standards: EN 13432. It requires the packaging to decompose within 12 weeks under industrial-scale composting conditions, leaving no more than 10% of the original material in clumps larger than 2 mm, and does not damage itself through heavy metal pollution or deteriorate its structure.
She started feeding waste edible oil to microorganisms to make PHA, but in recent years has been studying how to convert waste plastics such as polystyrene into new, biodegradable plastics. This is preferable to using freshly grown crops as raw materials because it saves plants that can be used as food while consuming waste plastic.
