When pipetting volumes from 0.2 to 5 µL, pipetting accuracy and precision is of utmost importance a good pipetting technique is essential because handling mistakes are more obvious with small volumes.
The following points are essential: Pipette and tip size: Always choose the pipette with the lowest nominal volume possible and the smallest tip to keep the air cushion as small as possible.
Small adjustments and broken parts on a pipette lead to a massive increase in systematic and random error values. A calibration according to ISO 8655 must be performed once a year.
Try to use larger volumes: You may consider diluting your sample to pipette larger volumes with the same quantity in the final reaction. This can reduce pipetting errors with very small sample volumes.
Tip attachment: Do not jam the pipette onto the tip as this may damage the fine tip end cause the liquid beam to be redirected or damage the orifice. Only apply light pressure when attaching a tip and use a pipette with a spring-loaded tip cone.
