The researchers found that a temperature of 100.4(F (38(℃) measured rectally could range anywhere from 98.6(F (37(℃) to 102.6(F (39.2(℃) when using an ear thermometer. Smyth says the results do not mean that infrared ear thermometers should be abandoned by pediatricians and parents, but rather that a single ear reading should not be used to determine the course of treatment.
“In my clinical experience the ear thermometer often gives a false reading, especially if a child has a very badear infection,” Walker tells WebMD. “A lot of parents are uncomfortable taking rectal temperatures, but I still feel that they are the best way to get an accurate reading.”
